From 600f068c7b5752d03c03594011d8b0853b80358e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 20:19:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 39/53] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, a lost device ends the acquire
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: vkAcquireNextImageKHR with timeout = UINT64_MAX could never
 return once the device was lost. The zero-copy acquire loops until a
 buffer frees, and the only conditions that end it were "a buffer
 arrived", "the caller's deadline passed" and "another swapchain took
 the window". None of those can happen when the device is dead and the
 application is holding every image: the presents that would hand them
 back fail, so the queue can never produce one, and an infinite
 timeout has no deadline to expire.
Evidence: hardware run 14 (2026-08-08, build 2026-08-08T18:48:25.691Z
 9b4f976 mesa:815dca2), t_vk_swapchain. The graphics channel took an
 MMU fault — "[horizon_gpu:E] channel 0x7a8f640010: fault notification
 31 (MMU fault) — marking lost" — after which every submit and fence
 wait returned VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST: "2 images, FIFO: 0 of 90 frames
 presented", "recording and submitting the frame at frame 0 ->
 DEVICE_LOST". The test then reached its infinite-timeout session and
 never came back: the log ends at "acquiring with timeout =
 UINT64_MAX for 20 frames" with no RESULT line, which is what a hang
 looks like from outside.
Upstream: no — this file is the Horizon backend itself.

Two checks, because two paths can fail to produce an image.

The blocking one is checked every round rather than once on entry: the
device can be lost while the loop is running, and that is the case
that hung. vk_device_is_lost is an atomic read of a flag NVK already
sets, so a round costs nothing extra.

The entry check covers the copy fallback as well, and it deliberately
runs before the VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR paths. The fallback never
blocks — it answers OUT_OF_DATE when every image is with the
application — but an application told to recreate its swapchain on a
dead device will recreate it and ask again. DEVICE_LOST is the more
fundamental answer and the only one that ends that loop.

What this does not do is explain the MMU fault. That is a separate and
open question: the channel faulted immediately after 120 pattern
frames had presented successfully, run 13 did not fault through the
same sequence, and nothing here can say why yet. This patch is about
what the driver does afterwards, which was to hang.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 5d6d85e2cbd..86ce94dc45b 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@
 #include "util/macros.h"
 #include "util/os_time.h"
 #include "util/u_debug.h"
+#include "vk_device.h"
 #include "vk_instance.h"
 #include "vk_log.h"
 #include "vk_physical_device.h"
@@ -1360,6 +1361,33 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
       if (!wsi_horizon_presentable(chain))
          return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
 
+      /* A LOST DEVICE ENDS THIS WAIT, and until run 14 it did not.
+       *
+       * Measured on hardware (2026-08-08, run 14, t_vk_swapchain): the
+       * graphics channel took an MMU fault, every submit and every
+       * fence wait afterwards returned VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST, and the
+       * application was left holding both images of a two-image
+       * swapchain because the presents that would have handed them back
+       * had failed. The next acquire therefore asked a queue that could
+       * never produce a buffer — and at timeout = UINT64_MAX there is
+       * no deadline left to expire, so it asked forever. The test hung
+       * with no verdict; a real application would hang the same way.
+       *
+       * The loop above cannot see any of that: `presentable` is about
+       * which swapchain owns the window, and the BufferQueue is alive
+       * and answering. What is dead is the device, and Vulkan has a
+       * result for exactly this — vkAcquireNextImageKHR may return
+       * VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST, which is both the truth and a value the
+       * caller can act on.
+       *
+       * Checked every round rather than once on entry, because the
+       * device can be lost while this loop is running, which is the
+       * case that hung. vk_device_is_lost is an atomic read of a flag
+       * NVK already sets. */
+      VK_FROM_HANDLE(vk_device, device, chain->base.device);
+      if (vk_device_is_lost(device))
+         return VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST;
+
       /* THE APPLICATION HOLDS EVERYTHING, so this is a deadlock and not
        * a wait — and the copy fallback has said so since 0053 while
        * this path did not. The same condition, detected on one path and
@@ -1499,6 +1527,16 @@ wsi_horizon_swapchain_acquire_next_image(struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
    struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain =
       (struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *)wsi_chain;
 
+   /* Before anything else, and before VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR in
+    * particular. Both paths need this, not only the one that blocks:
+    * the fallback answers OUT_OF_DATE when it cannot produce an image,
+    * and an application told to recreate a swapchain on a dead device
+    * will recreate it and ask again. DEVICE_LOST is the more
+    * fundamental answer and the only one that ends that loop. */
+   VK_FROM_HANDLE(vk_device, device, wsi_chain->device);
+   if (vk_device_is_lost(device))
+      return VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST;
+
    if (!wsi_horizon_presentable(chain)) {
       /* A newer swapchain took the window. Nothing acquired from here
        * could be presented. */
-- 
